1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.833
Running into issues with your report?

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Let's cover three common problems and how to fix them.

3
00:00:05.266 --> 00:00:07.632
Issue 1: Dimension conflicts.
 

4
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.633
If you select measures and dimensions that don't work together, you'll see an error.

5
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:13.933
For example,

6
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:17.766
"Battery level at shift start" doesn't work with "App version",

7
00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:20.666
because app versions aren't relevant to battery level.

8
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:24.399
To fix this, choose a dimension that's related to your measure.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.633
In this example, we can measure battery levels for a specific device group.

10
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.200
Issue 2: Line charts with multiple measures and dimension filters.

11
00:00:36.866 --> 00:00:39.232
Line charts support multiple measures 

12
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:42.833
only when your dimension filter is limited to a single value.

13
00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:45.927
For example, we can compare "Screen time", 

14
00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:50.432
"Foreground run time", and "Background run time" for a single app.

15
00:00:51.966 --> 00:00:54.099
But if you select more than one app,

16
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.833
you'll get an error because your measures conflict with the dimension filter.

17
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:03.066
The fix? Limit the dimension filter to a single value.

18
00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:07.166
Or, if you need more complexity, switch to a table report.

19
00:01:08.333 --> 00:01:12.533
Issue 3: Line charts with mismatched calculations.

20
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:16.033
When adding multiple measures to a line chart,

21
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:19.399
each measure must use the same calculation method.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:23.966
For example, if you're comparing "Abnormal" and "Force close" events,

23
00:01:23.966 --> 00:01:28.799
you can't compare the total for one with the average for the other.

24
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.133
Their calculation methods must match.

25
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.166
Some measures also support only one calculation method. 

26
00:01:36.166 --> 00:01:39.999
For example, if "Weak battery health" only supports "Sum",

27
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.600
then the other measures in your report must also use "Sum".

28
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.933
Remember, error messages are there to help!

29
00:01:48.933 --> 00:01:52.266
They can tell you what's wrong and often suggest how to fix it.

30
00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:56.666
Read the message, adjust your selections, and try again.

31
00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:57.866
 And that's a wrap.

32
00:01:58.433 --> 00:02:00.833
You now know how to troubleshoot common issues 

33
00:02:00.833 --> 00:02:04.399
when creating customized reports with dynamic data export.

34
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.466
To learn more,

35
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:10.999
visit the Dynamic Data Export documentation at docs.samsung.nox.com.

36
00:02:11.266 --> 00:02:12.432
Thanks for watching!

